1
00:00:01,220 --> 00:00:04,814
This month, a machine we built gets so far away that light needs a whole day to reach it.

2
00:00:04,884 --> 00:00:06,032
It's Voyager 1.

3
00:00:06,115 --> 00:00:08,351
It left Earth in 1977.

4
00:00:08,421 --> 00:00:13,477
It has been flying for almost fifty years, at about seventeen kilometres every second.

5
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:17,755
On November eighteenth, it passes twenty-five point nine billion kilometres.

6
00:00:17,888 --> 00:00:18,934
One light-day.

7
00:00:19,004 --> 00:00:21,619
If we say hello, it hears us a day later.

8
00:00:21,669 --> 00:00:23,575
Its answer takes another day to come back.

9
00:00:23,645 --> 00:00:28,498
Its radio is about as strong as a fridge light bulb, and our giant dishes still catch it.

10
00:00:28,575 --> 00:00:32,599
Most of its instruments are switched off now, to save its last bit of power.

11
00:00:32,669 --> 00:00:37,658
On board is a golden record, with music and greetings from Earth, in case anyone ever finds it.

12
00:00:38,008 --> 00:00:39,076
But here's the thing.

13
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:41,358
The nearest star isn't one light-day away.

14
00:00:41,428 --> 00:00:43,031
It's more than fifteen hundred.

15
00:00:43,114 --> 00:00:46,802
At this speed, Voyager would need over seventy thousand years to cross that gap.
